    "markdown": "# bezout-identity-oq-03-oq-05: Garner's Algorithm (Mixed-Radix CRT)\n\n## Problem\n\nExtend the two-modulus Bézout CRT solver `crtInt` (bezout-identity-oq-03) to `k` pairwise-coprime moduli by formalizing **Garner's algorithm** — the classical mixed-radix reconstruction — and proving it returns the canonical CRT solution.\n\n## Outcome: COMPLETE (0 sorries, 0 axioms)\n\nThe algorithm builds `x = v₁ + v₂·m₁ + v₃·m₁m₂ + ⋯ + v_k·m₁⋯m_{k-1}` in a single left-to-right sweep carrying only the running value `x` and partial product `P`. At step `j`: `vⱼ = (rⱼ − x)·(P⁻¹ mod mⱼ)` reduced into `[0, mⱼ)`, then `x ← x + vⱼ·P`, `P ← P·mⱼ`.\n\n### Key idea\n\nThe per-step modular inverse `P⁻¹ mod mⱼ` is realized **without** any `ZMod.inv` plumbing: the Bézout cofactor `Int.gcdA P mⱼ` already satisfies `P · Int.gcdA P mⱼ ≡ 1 [ZMOD mⱼ]` — the very identity behind the parent `crtInt`. The `% m` reduction is what keeps the digits and the running value bounded, the defining feature of Garner's form.\n\n### Results\n\n- `garner_modEq` — every congruence `(mᵢ, rᵢ)` is satisfied.\n- `garner_range` — `0 ≤ garner < ∏ mᵢ` (genuine mixed-radix bound).\n- `garner_unique` — any `y ∈ [0, ∏ mᵢ)` satisfying all congruences equals `garner`.\n\nAll proofs sorry-free and axiom-free (only Lean's foundational axioms). The arithmetic core is a single Bézout step, reused from the gallery's `crtInt`.\n"
  },
